==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*h₂el-|id=grow}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*alō}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*h₂életi||grow, nourish}}. Related to {{m|la|*oleō}}.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|page=35}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|alō|eccl=1}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|3|alō|alu|alt/alit}}

# to [[encourage]] or [[promote]] the [[development]] (of a thing): to [[foster]], to [[further]], to [[promote]] (something)
#: {{syn|la|foveō|iuvō|sublevō}}
# to cause (a thing) to [[continue]] over time: to [[further]], to [[maintain]], to [[sustain]]
#: {{syn|la|subsistō|sufferō|sustentō|sustineō}}
#* Attributed to [[w:Cato the Elder|Cato Maior]] by [[w:Livy|Livius]] in [[w:Ab Urbe Condita Libri|Ab Urbe Condita]], Book XXXIV, 9.12
#*: ''Bellum sē ipsum '''alet'''''.
#*:: The war '''will further''' itself.
# {{sense|in a particular sense}} to [[feed]], to [[nourish]], to [[nurture]]
#: {{syn|la|nūtriō|sagīnō|pāsco}}
# {{sense|pertaining to living things}} to [[cultivate]], to [[raise]], to [[rear]], etc. (as a child, an animal, etc.)
#: {{ux|la|[[homo|Hominum]] [[gratia]] [[genero|generatur]], '''aluntur''' [[bestia|bestiae]].|It is for the sake of man that beasts are '''bred'''.}}

====Usage notes====
''Alō'' refers to the transitive act of causing someone or something to grow or develop. There was a companion lost stative to ''alō'' in Old Latin {{l|la|*aleō|t=I grow up, I develop, I mature}}, which remained effective in Classical Latin through its derived verb {{l|la|alēscō|t= I grow, I grow up, I increase}}.

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|3|alō|alu|alt/alit}}

====Derived terms====
